# Harnesses Are Just Brain Tricks

The chronology of agent harnessing shares a lot with the chronology of human knowledge tooling.

               Problem       Agent solution       Human solution                       Agents cannot effectively review their own work       Get another agent to do the review       Peer review                          Context windows are not big enough to work on long problems              Get agents to write things down       Notetaking                 Agents cannot work effectively on very big problems                Allow agents to invoke (networks of) subagents              Make companies, organizations                          Agents cannot do many / very complex things without being told how              Skills       Textbooks, tutorials, trainings                 Agents can waffle around       Leave them alone       Don't micromanage                          Agents don't know enough about the user to work effectively              Connectors       Research, cross-referencing                          Agents sometimes do high-impact things they shouldn't                       Sandbox the agent but allow escape with approval              Security clearance & controls         

I think you can reasonably predict future harness improvements by following human knowledge tooling improvements:

               Human Pattern       Agent Prediction       AKA                                Hierarchies are helpful, but so is talking laterally              Agents will talk with sibling agents       Direct messaging                          As communication increases, so does desync                       Agents will join highly targeted and temporary multiplayer agent         chatrooms              Meetings                          People get better results when they take time away from doing work to         think (actively or passively) about work more zoomed-out                       Agents will benefit from being run between goals without a stated         problem to solve              Time off                          Skill creation can be selfish or prosocial, not just initiated by the         top of the hierarchy                       Agents will create skills without being asked and share them with other         agents without being asked              Innovate & ask for help                          In large organizations people develop reputations, norms, mentorships,         status                       As larger groups of agents work together, it will be helpful for them to         develop internal judgements of other agents and sometimes share these         with other agents selectively              Gossip                          Agents can be spun up and/or reused by the human, and spin-down is a         sign of non-helpfulness                       Each replacement agent can slightly tweak its behavior from its         predecessor, with some amount of randomness, to lead to better outcomes         for the human and therefore a longer lifetime for the agent              Natural selection         
